      <span class="section-label">Where the money comes back, line by line</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">Four places the fee earns itself back.</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle" style="margin-inline:auto">The $175 does not pay itself back in one mysterious way. It pays itself back in four specific ways at once, and any one of them is usually enough on its own. The other three are upside on top.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:0">1. New leads from a site that actually loads.</h2>
    <p>The single largest line on the return side is the leads your current site is quietly losing. Mobile visitors abandon a page after roughly three seconds of waiting, and Google's own research, repeatedly confirmed by independent studies, places the bounce uplift at seven to ten percent for every additional second of load time. A custom-coded site at 95+ PageSpeed loads in about one second; a typical Wix, Squarespace, or unmaintained WordPress site loads in four or five.</p>
    <p>The math on a small service business that does roughly one thousand monthly visitors, with seventy percent of them on mobile and a two-percent form-conversion rate, comes out to somewhere between eight and twenty extra leads a year just from retaining the visits the slow site was losing before they ever saw the headline. At a forty-percent close rate and a five-hundred-dollar average job, that is between sixteen hundred and four thousand dollars in recovered revenue, on the leakage line alone. The fee is paid off and then some, before any of the other three lines are counted. The <a href="/calculator/" class="inline-link">lead-leakage calculator</a> lets you plug in your own numbers and see the figure for yourself.</p>

    <h2>2. Replaced line items you are already paying for.</h2>
    <p>The flat $175 is structured to absorb the cost of the four or five recurring vendors most service-business owners already pay separately, which means a meaningful share of the fee is not a new expense at all. It is the same expense, consolidated, and the consolidation itself usually saves money once the small invoices are added up honestly.</p>
    <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.5rem 0">
      <table class="compare-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">What you might be paying separately</th><th scope="col">Typical monthly cost</th><th scope="col">Inside the $175 plan</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Website hosting (managed WordPress, Wix Business, Squarespace Business)</td><td>$25&ndash;$60</td><td class="yes">Included</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SSL certificate, CDN, and DDoS protection</td><td>$10&ndash;$25</td><td class="yes">Included</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Security plugin or malware-scanning subscription</td><td>$15&ndash;$40</td><td class="yes">Included; no plugins to patch</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Backup and uptime monitoring</td><td>$10&ndash;$25</td><td class="yes">Included</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Premium theme, page builder, or form plugin licenses</td><td>$15&ndash;$30</td><td class="yes">Not needed; custom-coded</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hourly developer or VA for content edits</td><td>$50&ndash;$200</td><td class="yes">Unlimited content updates, included</td></tr>
