        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The honest reading of the table above is that even before counting a single new lead, most clients arrive at the engagement already paying somewhere between sixty and two hundred dollars a month for the same infrastructure, split across four or five small invoices. Folding those into a single line item at $175 is usually a wash on cost and a clear win on attention, since you stop tracking five renewals and start tracking one.</p>

    <h2>3. The agency invoice you do not have to write.</h2>
    <p>The traditional alternative to a flat monthly plan is a regional agency, and the regional agency model is a three- to seven-thousand-dollar design deposit at signing, followed by a hosting and maintenance retainer that usually lands somewhere between one hundred and three hundred dollars a month, with every meaningful change after launch billed at one hundred and fifty to two hundred dollars an hour. The first year, on that model, costs five to ten thousand dollars before a single edit is requested.</p>
    <p>The flat plan eliminates the deposit entirely, folds the retainer into the same $175, and includes the change requests that the agency would have metered. The total annual cost on this plan is $2,100. On the agency model, with three or four content updates over the year, the comparable annual cost lands between four and seven thousand dollars on the low end. The delta is real money, and it does not get billed.</p>

    <h2>4. SEO that compounds, instead of decays.</h2>
    <p>A site that ranks for "[your service] near [your city]" earns leads continuously, at no incremental ad spend, for as long as it is maintained. The local-SEO foundation, real schema, real area pages, real internal linking, is built into every site I ship, and it is maintained quietly in the background as the site grows. The compounding effect is the line on the return side that is hardest to quantify in a calculator, and it is also the line that tends to dwarf the others by year two or three.</p>
    <p>A single client of mine in a competitive trade went from invisible in the local pack to consistently in the top three results within six months, on the strength of the schema and area-page work alone. The honest framing is that I do not promise rankings, since nobody honest does. The honest framing is that I build the site so that ranking is structurally possible, then keep maintaining it so the ranking does not decay.</p>

