      <span class="section-label">What if the site brings in zero new leads?</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">The fee still pays for itself.</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle" style="margin-inline:auto">It is fair to ask the inverse question: what if the new site, somehow, generates not a single additional lead? It is a low-probability scenario, since faster pages and better local rankings almost always lift call volume at the margin, but it is the right scenario to test the floor on.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Even in the zero-new-leads case, the $175 still beats the alternative. Hosting, SSL, security, monitoring, plugin licenses, and an hourly developer for content edits, taken together, run somewhere between fifteen hundred and three thousand dollars a year for the average service-business owner who is paying for them piecemeal. The plan folds those into the same monthly figure, and the fee is therefore at worst a wash on cost and at best a meaningful saving, before any leads are counted at all.</p>
    <p>Put a different way, you are not paying $175 a month for leads. You are paying $175 a month for the infrastructure you already need, the maintenance you already need, and the support you already need, and the leads are the upside the structure happens to produce on top.</p>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--cream">
