    <div class="text-center" style="max-width:720px;margin:0 auto 2.5rem">
      <span class="section-label">A few honest caveats</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">Where the math does not work.</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle" style="margin-inline:auto">The fee does not pay itself back in every situation, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest. There are a small handful of cases where the structure is the wrong fit, and I will tell you so on the discovery call before either of us signs anything.</p>
    </div>

    <ul style="font-size:1.02rem;line-height:1.75">
      <li style="margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>Your business is too new to have a baseline.</strong> If you have not yet been in business long enough to know your average job value or your typical lead volume, there is no math to compare against, and the right move is usually to start cheaper and re-evaluate in twelve months. I will tell you so.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>You have technical skill in-house.</strong> If you or someone on your team can read code, configure a CDN, patch plugins, and write SEO-clean pages, the consolidated plan is paying for labor you can already do yourself. The freelance route or self-hosted route is often the better fit. The <a href="/vs-freelancer/" class="inline-link">freelancer comparison</a> walks through the trade-off.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>Your trade does not depend on local search.</strong> A purely word-of-mouth business that never gets a single web lead has no leakage to recover from a faster site. The infrastructure savings still hold, but the lead-generation line collapses, and the math is less compelling.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>You need the site primarily as a brochure.</strong> If the site is genuinely a static one-pager with no maintenance need, a one-off freelance build can be cheaper over five years, even with the eventual rebuild factored in. I would not pitch the plan against a real brochure-only need.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">The honest framing of the entire arrangement is that the $175 is structured to be a good deal for the specific category of small service business that depends on the website for any meaningful share of its lead flow. Everyone else has a better option somewhere, and if you are one of those, the discovery call is the right place to find that out without spending anything.</p>

  </div>
</section>

